addition to <a href="/doc/84453334/" id="a_192">Section 53</a> of the IBC. The State contended that the non-obstante
clause in the state enactment and the non-obstante clause in the IBC operated at
different fields, and the State had to be treated as a ‘secured creditor’ by virtue of
<a href="/doc/49099016/" id="a_193">Section 48</a> of the state act. This was rejected by the NCLT and the NCLAT.</span>
</p><span class="hidden_text" id="span_101">                                            30</span>

<p data-structure="Precedent" id="p_99">However, this court took note of <a href="/doc/136877526/" id="a_194">Sections 30</a> and <a href="/doc/189958158/" id="a_195">31</a> of the IBC and certain other
provisions and held that NCLT had erred in its observations. It was held that:
